ower at vendors. Feedback in our research suggests vendor inventory remains in 4-5 moth range (4-6 months in Sept) - higher in NAND vs DRAM, and DDR5 at relatively normal levels. Most expect recent underutilization to result in more normal supplier inventory by 2Q24. Customer inventory appears mixed, with hyperscale inventory of DDR4/NAND still elevated (10+ weeks in some cases) and PC/Server OEM inventory more balanced. In Hyperscale, we hear Amazon digestion could continue through 1H24, and recent feedback also suggests Google orders remain soft & inventory higher, with Meta/Microsoft comparatively better. We see some efforts on the OEM side to buy-ahead a few weeks of DRAM and NAND inventory given low ASPs - we do not yet see this as a significant risk to future demand. Inventory at China mobile OEMs likely higher due to Micron-ban related buy ahead and expectations Huawei re-emergence negatively impacts OVX units in 2H23 and 2024 (also seen as a headwind to Apple).## Image 2 description:This image is a bar chart titled **\\\"OEM Customer Inventory Levels: Weeks-on-hand near quarter-end\\\"** and represents inventory levels for **DRAM** (Dynamic Random-Access Memory) over several quarters. Here's a detailed analysis of its components:### Axes:- **X-axis (horizontal):** Represents time in quarterly intervals, starting from the first quarter of 2018 (1Q18) to the first quarter of 2024 (1Q24e). The \\\"e\\\" in later quarters (e.g., 3Q23e, 1Q24e) likely signifies **estimated** or forecasted data.- **Y-axis (vertical):** Represents the inventory level in \\\"weeks-on-hand,\\\" ranging from 0 to 14.### Bars:- The blue bars indicate the DRAM inventory levels (in weeks-on-hand) for each quarter shown.### Red Dashed Lines:- Two horizontal red dashed lines are drawn across the chart at approximately **6 weeks** and **8 weeks**. These likely represent thresholds or benchmarks for inventory levels:  - **6 weeks:** Could represent a lower threshold or minimum inventory level.  - **8 weeks:** Could represent an upper threshold or ideal inventory level.### Observations and Trends:1. **2018-2019:** Inventory levels were relatively low, staying below 6 weeks for most quarters. The only exception is 3Q18, where inventory briefly exceeded 8 weeks.2. **2020:** Inventory levels varied widely, with significant increases in 1Q20 and 2Q20 (above 8 weeks), but a decline in 3Q20 and 4Q20 (near or below 6 weeks).3. **2021-2022:** Inventory levels rose consistently, peaking in **3Q22** at approximately 13 weeks (the highest point in the chart), significantly exceeding the 8-week threshold.4. **2023-2024 (estimated):**   - Inventory levels are forecasted to decrease steadily, falling below 8 weeks by 3Q23e and reaching around 6 weeks by 1Q24e.### Key Insights:- DRAM inventory levels have fluctuated significantly over the years, with notable peaks in 1Q20 and 3Q22.- The forecast indicates an effort to bring inventory levels closer to the red dashed lines, particularly the 6-8 week range, suggesting an attempt to stabilize inventory.- High inventory levels in 2022 could reflect supply chain disruptions, overproduction, or reduced demand, while the projected decline in 2023-2024 may indicate normalization or adjustment to demand.### Additional Notes:- The asterisk (*) next to the title suggests there may be additional notes or caveats about the data not visible in the chart.- This chart is useful for analyzing trends in DRAM inventory management by OEMs over time.Source: Cleveland Research Company estimates\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"MarketProvider\": \"Cleveland Research Company\",\n    \"FileName\": \"DRAM Industry Themes (10-25-23).pdf\",\n    \"FileType\": \"pdf\",\n    \"FileUnit\": \"4\",\n    \"Content\": \"## APPENDIXDisclosures: We, Chandler Converse and Henry Gerlach, certify that the views expressed in the research report(s) accurately reflect our personal views about the subject security(s). Further, we certify that no part of our compensation was, 